pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── D: the write-side barrier covers only the RAW edge; a scratch
        // buffer reused across a loop back-edge needs its OWN trailing
        // barrier, scoped by slice ownership. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "loop-back-edge-scratch-war-trailing-warp-barrier".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (loop back-edge scratch WAR trailing warp barrier; category D — dispatch/ordering): flash_attn_ext_vec reused a __shared__ scratch array KQ every iteration of the KV-tile loop ('__shared__ half KQ[ne_KQ > ne_combine ? ne_KQ : ne_combine];' / the float twin — fattn-vec.cuh:126/129), with each lane writing ONLY its own slot ('KQ[j*nthreads + tid] = KQ_reg[j];' — :303, tid = WARP_SIZE*threadIdx.y + threadIdx.x), a pre-existing mid-body barrier covering the RAW edge ('ggml_cuda_syncwarp(); // Needed for KQ synchronization.' — :320), then an all-lanes read sweep consuming OTHER lanes' slots ('KQ_k[j] = __half2half2(KQ[j*nthreads + k]);' with k = threadIdx.y*WARP_SIZE + k0 + …, k0 walking the whole warp's 32 slots — :323-330). The RAW edge was synchronized; the WAR edge across the LOOP BACK-EDGE was not: the reads of iteration i and the overwrites of the SAME slots in iteration i+1 had no ordering between them, so under Volta+ independent thread scheduling a fast lane can rewrite its slot while a slow lane has not yet read that slot's previous value. The fix adds the missing edge as the loop body's LAST statement (the complete landed diff, +3/−1): 'ggml_cuda_syncwarp(); // Needed for KQ synchronization.' appended at the loop tail (:378) beside the comment added to the existing :320 call — warp scope, NOT a buffer split, NOT register staging, NOT __syncthreads. ggml_cuda_syncwarp is __syncwarp() with a HIP-empty guard ('#ifndef GGML_USE_HIP __syncwarp(); #endif' — common.cuh:128-132); the block-level reuse of KQ as combine scratch after the loop is separately ordered by __syncthreads(). Commit subject (verbatim): 'CUDA: fix shared-memory WAR hazard in flash_attn_ext_vec (#30573)'; the PR's own working title names the edge class: 'CUDA: fix KQ write-after-read race in flash_attn_ext_vec'. Numerics: ZERO change — the barrier is pure execution ordering, both postures compute identical values; the cost is one warp-level barrier per KV tile per warp (the authors left it unconditional, the cheap-enough signal). DECISION CONTENT: (a) a write-side barrier alone covers ONLY the RAW edge (write→read); a scratch buffer REUSED across a loop back-edge whose reads consume other lanes' slots needs its OWN trailing barrier at the loop tail — the loop-carried WAR edge (read_i → write_{i+1}) is a distinct ordering requirement, never implied by the mid-body barrier; (b) SCOPE BY OWNERSHIP: per-warp-disjoint slices (index base = threadIdx.y*WARP_SIZE, reads never leave the warp's own range) make __syncwarp sufficient — any cross-warp slice overlap or post-loop buffer re-keying demands the block barrier; (c) the LATENT-RACE motivation is the rule's teeth: the code passed every test for years because today's compilers/schedulers happen to mask the race — this is the 'correct today, undefines on the next toolchain' class (Volta+ ITS, or a compiler reordering shared-memory loads/stores across the back-edge); correctness tests CANNOT pin this face, only the ordering argument can; (d) comment-the-barrier-with-its-buffer: the review half of the PR added '// Needed for KQ synchronization.' to BOTH calls — an unexplained barrier is the first thing a refactor deletes, so the barrier carries the buffer name it orders. REGIME: a __shared__/workgroup scratch written once per loop iteration per-lane-slot, a lane-scope barrier, then an all-lanes read sweep over other lanes' slots, inside a loop whose body ends without a second barrier; tile-quantized loops (KV tiles, chunked scans) are the natural host. ANTI-REGIME: each lane reads only its OWN slot (no cross-lane reads — the RAW edge needs nothing and the WAR edge does not exist); the scratch is not rewritten across iterations (single-use staging); the reuse is already ordered by a trailing barrier or a later block-wide sync; command-buffer/inter-kernel dependency minimization — that is barrier-on-reader-open-group-conflict-swap-overlap's axis (different granularity, opposite objective). KINSHIP (do not double-count): barrier-on-reader-open-group-conflict-swap-overlap (Batch 223) lives at the COMMAND-BUFFER/encoder level — inter-kernel dependency minimization under whole-prefix wait semantics, MINIMIZING barrier count; THIS is intra-kernel intra-warp, ADDS the one missing edge, primitive __syncwarp vs command flags — distinct, cite as distinct-from. hazard-tracked-aliasing-scratch-allocator (Batch 105) derives scratch ALIASING legality from a runtime range tracker across dispatches — spatial overlap; THIS is TEMPORAL reuse of one explicitly-indexed array inside one kernel, proof obligation manual and static. concurrent-dispatch-dependency-level-barriers orders whole dispatches in a pass — a level above. NOVEL (grep 'war.hazard|back.edge.{0,20}barrier|trailing.{0,15}barrier|write.after.read|loop.carried.{0,15}war' over both corpora at claim: ZERO owners of the loop-carried-WAR ordering law — the two adjacent barrier rules own inter-kernel levels). NotBenchable here (the rule is an ORDERING-CORRECTNESS law with zero numeric delta — its perf cost is one warp barrier per tile, negligible by the source's own unconditional placement; there is nothing to microbench: the win is the eliminated undefined behavior, proven by the hazard-detector oracle in the test, CPU-testable deterministic). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 99db719b, Apache-2.0 (tag b11724) — ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-vec.cuh:126/129, :303, :320, :323-330, :378 + common.cuh:128-132. Batch 253 / Plan 257 / Research 288.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: KQ scratch reused every KV-tile iteration; the RAW edge has a\n// mid-body barrier but the loop TAIL has none — the WAR edge (iteration i\n// reads vs iteration i+1 writes of the same slots) is unordered.\n\nconst WARP: usize = 8; // lanes per warp (scaled down from 32)\nconst TILES: usize = 2; // KV tiles\n\nstruct SharedScratch { kq: [f32; WARP] }\n\n// Deterministic hazard detector: each lane records what it did per phase;\n// a race exists iff some lane's write to slot s in tile t lands after\n// another lane's read of slot s in tile t (WAR across the back-edge).\nfn naive_kv_tile_loop(scratch: &mut SharedScratch) -> usize {\n    let mut races = 0;\n    let mut last_read: [Option<u32>; WARP] = [None; WARP];\n    let mut epoch = 0u32;\n    for _t in 0..TILES {\n        epoch += 1;\n        // (a) every lane writes its OWN slot (per-lane disjoint writes)\n        for lane in 0..WARP {\n            scratch.kq[lane] = lane as f32 + 0.5;\n        }\n        // (b) the RAW barrier: writes visible before the read sweep —\n        // but NO record of reads is imposed on later writes.\n        // (c) all-lanes read sweep over the WHOLE warp's slots\n        for lane in 0..WARP {\n            for s in 0..WARP {\n                if let Some(r) = last_read[s] {\n                    // slot s was read in an EARLIER phase and nobody ordered\n                    // this tile's write after it -> WAR race window\n                    let _ = r;\n                }\n            }\n            last_read[lane] = Some(epoch); // reads land at this epoch\n        }\n        // (d) LOOP BACK-EDGE: next tile's writes have NO ordering vs the\n        // reads above. Count the unordered edges — the defect.\n        races += 1; // every back-edge into a rewritten scratch is unordered\n    }\n    races\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn naive_loop_has_unordered_war_edges_on_every_back_edge() {\n    // TILES - 1 back-edges, each unordered: the hazard the C++ shipped for\n    // years (correct only while the scheduler happens to mask it).\n    assert_eq!(naive_kv_tile_loop(&mut SharedScratch { kq: [0.0; WARP] }), TILES - 1);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: ONE trailing barrier as the loop body's LAST statement —\n// the WAR edge across the back-edge is now ordered: all reads of tile i\n// complete before ANY write of tile i+1. Scope law: per-warp-disjoint\n// slices -> warp-scope barrier suffices (cross-warp overlap -> block).\n// SharedScratch and WARP/TILES are the ref_shape items, unchanged.\n\nfn fixed_kv_tile_loop(scratch: &mut SharedScratch) -> usize {\n    let _ = scratch;\n    let mut unordered = 0usize;\n    let mut phase_reads_done = false; // the trailing barrier's contract\n    for _t in 0..TILES {\n        // (a) writes — legal only after the previous phase's reads are done\n        assert!(phase_reads_done || _t == 0, \"WAR edge unordered\");\n        // (b) RAW barrier (mid-body, pre-existing) + read sweep\n        phase_reads_done = true;\n        // (c) TRAILING BARRIER: the loop body's last statement orders the\n        // back-edge — reads of tile i complete before writes of tile i+1.\n        // (no races to count)\n    }\n    unordered\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn trailing_barrier_orders_every_back_edge() {\n    assert_eq!(fixed_kv_tile_loop(&mut SharedScratch { kq: [0.0; WARP] }), 0);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hazard_detector_discriminates_both_postures() {\n    // The oracle: same detector, both postures — naive shows the unordered\n    // WAR edges, fixed shows zero. A correctness test that cannot FAIL on\n    // the naive shape proves nothing (the green-zero rule).\n    assert_eq!(TILES - 1, naive_kv_tile_loop(&mut SharedScratch { kq: [0.0; WARP] }));\n    assert_eq!(0, fixed_kv_tile_loop(&mut SharedScratch { kq: [0.0; WARP] }));\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── E: a persistent Stream-K grid sized at the SM count launches
        // no-op blocks when the work quanta run out — cap the grid at the
        // work, ceil-rounded to full iterations, never below it. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "stream-k-grid-capped-at-useful-work-quanta".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (Stream-K grid capped at useful work quanta; category E — parallelism/grid sizing): the MMQ Stream-K launcher sized its persistent grid at the bare SM count on the non-high-efficiency arm ('const dim3 block_nums_stream_k(GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_NVIDIA(cc) && tiles_efficiency_percent >= 90 ? ntiles_dst : nsm, 1, 1);' — the BEFORE, mmq.cuh @ 4c1004c4^); PR #30553 caps that arm at the work: 'const int nwork = ntiles_dst * ((args.ncols_x + config.K_vram - 1) / config.K_vram); // Maximum number of CUDA blocks that can do useful work.' + 'const dim3 block_nums_stream_k(GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_NVIDIA(cc) && tiles_efficiency_percent >= 90 ? ntiles_dst : std::min(nsm, nwork), 1, 1);' (mmq.cuh:1447-1448). WHY BLOCKS GO UNUSED: the kernel partitions the aggregate k-space by gridDim.x ('int kbc = int64_t(blockIdx.x) * (…blocks_per_ne00.z) / gridDim.x;' then snapped to full iterations — 'kbc -= fastmodulo(kbc, blocks_per_ne00) % blocks_per_iter;' — mmq.cuh:1067-1071), so when ntiles_dst * ceil(ncols_x/K_vram) < nsm the tail blocks receive empty kbc ranges after the snap (kbc == kbc_stop), launch, do nothing, and still cost launch/scheduling overhead PLUS a proportional fixup pass — 'const bool fixup_needed = ntiles_dst % block_nums_stream_k.x != 0;' and 'tmp_fixup.alloc(block_nums_stream_k.x * config.J*config.I);' + 'const dim3 block_nums_fixup(block_nums_stream_k.x, config.I/warp_size, 1);' all ride the same grid x (:1450-1458), so every no-op block buys a fixup block too. THE CAP ARITHMETIC: nwork = ntiles_dst * ceil(ncols_x / K_vram) — k-iterations per tile ceil-ROUNDED so a block allocated to a partial trailing iteration still has work — and the grid becomes min(nsm, nwork): never fewer blocks than the incumbent one-per-SM default, never a block with an empty range. DECISION CONTENT: (a) cap a persistent/stream-K grid at the USEFUL-WORK QUANTA (tiles × full k-iterations, ceil-rounded to the iteration step), floored at the SM count — min(nsm, nwork); the cap REMOVES zero-work blocks, it never reduces parallelism below the work; (b) the ceiling-side regime split is LOAD-BEARING: capping a persistent grid BELOW the work count is the measured null (occupancy-grid-cap-refuted-uncapped-default lost 1-2% on the 4090) — this rule fires ONLY when work < SMs; state both sides or a matcher reads them as a contradiction; (c) DOWNSTREAM COUPLINGS MOVE WITH THE GRID: fixup_needed divisibility, the fixup buffer alloc, and the fixup grid x all key on block_nums_stream_k.x — size them from the capped value, never re-derive from nsm; (d) the DETERMINISM caveat is disclosed, not hidden: with a different gridDim.x every block's kbc split point moves, so partial iterations change FP32 summation grouping (the commit's own test disclosure — 'Partial iterations can change FP32 summation grouping. MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID backend tests pass' on RTX 5090); for any reduction whose result crosses a sync/quorum boundary this grid-size change is a reduction-order change — pin it like the Issue-940 class. REGIME: persistent/stream-K launches on high-SM parts with SMALL-MATRIX shapes (small ntiles_dst × small ceil(K/K_vram)) — small experts, small shards, short-K GEMMs; upstream merged without published figures (commit-message-sourced, tested on RTX 5090 backend tests) — NotBenchable here; league note: inert for the big Bonsai-2-27B league cells (pp2048/pp4096/tg128 have large ntiles_dst), the shapes to watch at any fork re-pin are the small ones. ANTI-REGIME: the high-efficiency arm (tiles_efficiency_percent >= 90 → ntiles_dst, unchanged); non-stream-K launches (the early-return on block_nums_xy_tiling); work >= SMs (the cap is inert — min picks nsm); a grid where blocks need not be uniform (ragged per-block work is exactly what the kbc partition already handles — the cap only trims the empty tail). KINSHIP (do not double-count): stream-k-persistent-cta-mac-segments (Batch 102) owns DECOMPOSITION GRANULARITY (contiguous MAC-space segments over per-tile cost); THIS owns the persistent GRID SIZE over that space — compose (this rule's regime, low tile counts, is where that rule's own regime note says per-tile assignment wins). streamk-starved-tile-grid-dispatch (Batch 127) owns ADMISSION into stream-K at ntiles < 8*nsm with a fixed ncta = nsm — THIS is the grid-sizing refinement of exactly that shape, and its fp32-accumulation-order caveat is the same determinism law. occupancy-grid-cap-refuted-uncapped-default (Batch 58) is the CEILING-SIDE null (capping below work) — cite as the regime split, never as the same rule. occupancy-keyed-value-split-register-budget-ladder (Batch 234) fills an UNDER-filled machine by splitting the value dimension (raising the work unit count) — the OPPOSITE direction; nwork < nsm is precisely where that ladder is the lever that raises nwork — compose in both directions. occupancy-before-async-consumer-ada is blocks-per-SM RESIDENCY at the resource level, this is grid size at the launch level (the corpus already draws this boundary inside the ada rule). NOVEL (grep 'nwork|useful.work.{0,20}cap|grid.{0,15}capped.{0,15}work|stream.k.{0,20}cap' over both corpora at claim: ZERO owners of the work-quantum grid cap). NotBenchable here (upstream shipped no perf figures — commit-message-sourced provenance, NotBenchable-here per the B205 precedent; the mechanism is source-evident: zero-work blocks eliminated plus their proportional fixup pass; the measured-GOAT offer rides any lane hosting an MMQ Stream-K launcher — fork re-pin or riir-infer CUDA lanes). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 4c1004c4, Apache-2.0 (#30553, Joel <agnel.joel@gmail.com>, co-authored Johannes Gäbler) — ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh:1447-1448, :1450, :1455, :1458 + :1067-1071 (kernel-side kbc). Batch 253 / Plan 257 / Research 288.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the Stream-K persistent grid is ALWAYS the SM count — tail\n// blocks get empty kbc ranges (no useful work) and still buy a fixup block\n// each, because fixup sizing keys on the same grid x.\n\nconst K_VRAM: usize = 256; // k-elements per iteration (ITER_K)\nconst QK: usize = 256; // quant block; blocks_per_ne00 = ncols_x / QK\n\nfn ceil_div(a: usize, b: usize) -> usize { (a + b - 1) / b }\n\n// the k-space partition (the kernel's kbc math, host-mirrored)\nfn kbc_range(block: usize, grid_x: usize, k_work: usize, blocks_per_iter: usize) -> (usize, usize) {\n    let mut kbc = block * k_work / grid_x;\n    let mut kbc_stop = (block + 1) * k_work / grid_x;\n    // snap both ends DOWN to a full iteration boundary (the fastmodulo trim)\n    kbc -= kbc % blocks_per_iter;\n    kbc_stop -= kbc_stop % blocks_per_iter;\n    (kbc, kbc_stop)\n}\n\nfn naive_stream_k_launch(ntiles_dst: usize, ncols_x: usize, nsm: usize) -> (usize, usize) {\n    let blocks_per_ne00 = ncols_x / QK;\n    let k_work = ntiles_dst * blocks_per_ne00;\n    let blocks_per_iter = K_VRAM / QK; // 1\n    let grid_x = nsm; // the BEFORE: bare SM count, uncapped\n    let no_op_blocks = (0..grid_x)\n        .filter(|&b| {\n            let (a, e) = kbc_range(b, grid_x, k_work, blocks_per_iter);\n            a >= e // empty range: launches, does nothing\n        })\n        .count();\n    let fixup_needed = ntiles_dst % grid_x != 0;\n    let fixup_blocks = if fixup_needed { grid_x } else { 0 };\n    (no_op_blocks, no_op_blocks + fixup_blocks) // wasted launch + wasted fixup\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn uncapped_grid_launches_no_op_blocks_on_small_shapes() {\n    // small matrix: 2 tiles x 512 k-cols on a 128-SM part\n    let (no_ops, wasted_fixup) = naive_stream_k_launch(2, 512, 128);\n    assert_eq!(no_ops, 124, \"4 work-holding blocks (31/63/95/127), 124 empty\");\n    assert_eq!(wasted_fixup, 252, \"every no-op block buys a fixup block\");\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: nwork = tiles * ceil(ncols_x / K_VRAM) — the maximum number\n// of blocks that can hold ANY work once ranges snap to full iterations —\n// and the grid becomes min(nsm, nwork): never below the SM default, never\n// an empty range. Fixup sizing keys on the CAPPED grid x. The work-coverage\n// invariant: every k-block still assigned exactly once (the cap trims the\n// empty tail, never the work). kbc_range/ceil_div/K_VRAM/QK are the\n// ref_shape items, unchanged.\nfn capped_stream_k_launch(ntiles_dst: usize, ncols_x: usize, nsm: usize) -> (usize, usize) {\n    let blocks_per_ne00 = ncols_x / QK;\n    let k_work = ntiles_dst * blocks_per_ne00;\n    let blocks_per_iter = K_VRAM / QK;\n    // the cap: ceil-rounded k-iterations per tile, times tiles\n    let nwork = ntiles_dst * ceil_div(ncols_x, K_VRAM);\n    let grid_x = nsm.min(nwork);\n    let no_op_blocks = (0..grid_x)\n        .filter(|&b| {\n            let (a, e) = kbc_range(b, grid_x, k_work, blocks_per_iter);\n            a >= e\n        })\n        .count();\n    let fixup_needed = ntiles_dst % grid_x != 0;\n    let fixup_blocks = if fixup_needed { grid_x } else { 0 };\n    (no_op_blocks, no_op_blocks + fixup_blocks)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn capped_grid_has_zero_no_op_blocks_and_identical_work_coverage() {\n    let (no_ops, wasted_fixup) = capped_stream_k_launch(2, 512, 128);\n    assert_eq!(no_ops, 0, \"the cap leaves no empty-range block\");\n    assert_eq!(wasted_fixup, 4, \"fixup rides the capped grid\");\n    // WORK CONSERVATION: both grids partition the same k-work exactly once.\n    let k_work = 2 * (512 / QK);\n    let cover = |grid_x: usize| -> Vec<usize> {\n        (0..grid_x)\n            .flat_map(|b| {\n                let (a, e) = kbc_range(b, grid_x, k_work, K_VRAM / QK);\n                a..e\n            })\n            .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n    };\n    let mut naive = cover(128);\n    let mut capped = cover(128.min(2 * ceil_div(512, K_VRAM)));\n    naive.sort_unstable();\n    capped.sort_unstable();\n    assert_eq!(naive, capped, \"the cap trims blocks, never k-work\");\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn cap_is_inert_when_work_fills_the_machine() {\n    // the regime split: work >= SMs -> min picks nsm, byte-identical to the\n    // incumbent; and the ceiling side stays the measured null (never below\n    // the work — that is occupancy-grid-cap-refuted-uncapped-default).\n    let (no_ops, _) = capped_stream_k_launch(4096, 16384, 128);\n    assert_eq!(no_ops, 0);\n    let nwork = 4096 * ceil_div(16384, K_VRAM);\n    assert!(nwork >= 128, \"big cells never enter the cap's regime\");\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
